We can rewrite the divisor as $2x - 6 = 2(x - 3)$. Then, we can use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when $6x^4-14x^3-4x^2+2x-26$ is divided by $2x - 6$.
The Remainder Theorem states that if we divide a polynomial $f(x)$ by a linear divisor of the form $x - c$, then the remainder is equal to $f(c)$.
In this case, we have $f(x) = 6x^4-14x^3-4x^2+2x-26$ and $c = 3$. So, we need to evaluate $f(3)$.
\begin{align*}
f(3) &= 6(3)^4 - 14(3)^3 - 4(3)^2 + 2(3) - 26 \\
&= 6(81) - 14(27) - 4(9) + 6 - 26 \\
&= 486 - 378 - 36 + 6 - 26 \\
&= \boxed{10}
\end{align*}
The remainder when $6x^4-14x^3-4x^2+2x-26$ is divided by $2x - 6$ is $\boxed{10}$.The answer is: 10
